# Observability and Operations

Spec: `docs/context/OPERATIONS_RUNBOOK.md`.

## Readiness

Three independent levels: `LIVE_SAFE` (every confirmed position protected and
reconcilable), `ENTRY_READY` (feeds, broker, risk, storage, clock and config
valid for new exposure), `RESEARCH_READY`.

Entry readiness is evaluated per strategy dependency, not from one process
heartbeat. Research failure never removes live safety.

## Remediation

All remediation is bounded deterministic code or an explicit operator command.
AI may summarize evidence; it cannot invoke an action.

Safety controls are independently callable and tested: entry freeze, strategy
halt, cancel pending, controlled flatten, global halt. Each records actor,
scope, trigger, old and new state, command, result, broker reference and
incident ID.

Flattening is not the universal first response. An unreliable broker connection
makes repeated liquidation attempts dangerous.

## Monitoring

Feed and feature age, gaps, clock drift, heartbeat, storage durability, broker
connection, unknown/reject/partial counts, reconciliation lag, exposure and
margin discrepancy, protective coverage, AI cost and schema failures.

Keep IDs and symbols out of metric labels; put them in structured logs and
traces. Every critical alert has an owner, a severity and a tested runbook.

## Configuration governance

Versioned, validated, checksummed. A live change requires schema validation,
evidence, an approval record, a deployment record and a rollback target.
Volatile market rules live here, never in source constants. Secrets never
appear in prompts, logs or configuration files.
